[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Distributed Consensus (Paxos and Raft) in NoSQL Concepts"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Distributed NoSQL databases rely on replication to ensure high availability and fault tolerance. However, keeping multiple nodes synchronized requires a robust distributed consensus algorithm. This tutorial explores the two fundamental consensus protocols: Paxos and Raft."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"The Need for Consensus in NoSQL"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a distributed NoSQL system, nodes communicate over an unreliable network. Failures, network partitions, and delays are inevitable. Consensus algorithms ensure that a cluster of nodes can agree on a single data state or transaction log, even if some nodes fail."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Paxos: The Gold Standard of Consensus"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Paxos, introduced by Leslie Lamport, is the foundational protocol for distributed consensus. It operates through a series of phases: Prepare/Promise and Accept/Accepted. While theoretically robust, Paxos is notoriously difficult to understand and implement in real-world systems."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Proposers: Nodes that advocate for client commands."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Acceptors: Nodes that act as the memory of the protocol, voting on proposals."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Learners: Nodes that absorb the agreed-upon consensus state."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Raft: Understandability and Practicality"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Raft was designed specifically to be more understandable than Paxos. It decomposes consensus into distinct sub-problems: Leader Election, Log Replication, and Safety. Modern NoSQL databases like Apache Cassandra (using Paxos variants) and etcd (using Raft) leverage these principles."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leader: Handles all client requests and log replication."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Follower: Completely passive nodes that respond to requests from leaders and candidates."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Candidate: Used during elections to gather votes for a new leader."},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Code Example: Simulating a Raft State Machine in Pseudocode"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"class RaftNode {
    constructor(id) {
        this.id = id;
        this.state = "Follower";
        this.currentTerm = 0;
        this.votedFor = null;
        this.log = [];
    }

    startElection() {
        this.state = "Candidate";
        this.currentTerm += 1;
        this.votedFor = this.id;
        // Broadcast RequestVote RPC to peers
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"heading","text":"Summary"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Both Paxos and Raft solve the fundamental problem of distributed consensus, but Raft's modular approach has made it the preferred choice for many modern distributed NoSQL and coordination services."}]
